From f6ead370c0ea51ee4b5f75b6e337e67f7e4004b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 14:36:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(rs): a merge does not keep a fixed token either side
 removed, as in TypeScript

TypeScript's configure() deletes a fixed-token binding set to null from
the live option tree (`clean`, ts/src/utility.ts), so the tree
mergeOptionTrees reads holds no removal: the merge takes the binding the
other side holds, and new Tabnas(merged) builds over the defaults, which
puts back a default binding neither side holds. The Rust merge treated a
missing default fixed token as a deletion that wins against the default,
so css (which removes #OS and #CS) merged with jsonic lexed `[` as text
where TypeScript's merge lexes it as #OS, and a removal met by a rebinding
on the other side was refused as a conflict where TypeScript takes the
rebinding.

merge_fixed_tokens now takes every binding either side holds (picking
between two as before), puts back the defaults neither side holds, and
where a default put back and a held binding claim one source lets the
later of the two in default-first order keep it, as configure()'s
source map does. Two held bindings claiming one source are still refused
in merge_token_space, with TypeScript's message. Every other option map
keeps its removals, as TypeScript's null-valued keys do, and a removed
rule is still the other side's rule.

rs/tests/merge_test.rs pins it in all four merge orders (three tests fail
before this change); ts/test/merge.test.js pins the same results on the
canonical engine.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
